# The `build-image` tool (caos SPEC, "Tools"): assemble the submittable policy
# image around a binary `build-player` already compiled.
#
# The docs are a HERE-STRING, so editing them re-keys this tool's arg tree and
# NOTHING it builds.
HELP=<<END
Assemble the submittable policy image from the binary `build-player` produces,
and return its ref. This is the payoff of both stages of
players/baseline/Dockerfile sitting on one nixpkgs pin: the ccache-backed caos
build and the shipped image are the same compile, so this tool adds ONE ~1 MB
layer to the published runtime base rather than compiling anything.

The result is a git-docker delta — a `base` ref plus the policy layer — which
the caos server converts into an image digest. The 68 MB base is named, never
copied: it does not pass through the CAS, and a policy rebuild re-keys only its
own layer.
@param [player] Which policy under players/ to build. Default: baseline.
@param [defines] Extra defines, passed through to build-player, e.g. "-d:taunt".
@param [runtime] Runtime base to layer onto. Default: the tag players/baseline/Dockerfile names, which is the nixpkgs pin both it and caos/nim share.
END
curry --base:@=DEEP-DEPS/imgtools --worker1:@=worker.sh --nim:@=DEEP-DEPS/nim --help=$HELP
